# Go — project standards

## Package documentation

See [.cursor/rules/package-docs.mdc](package-docs.mdc): every significant package has a `README.md` next to the code; the root [README.md](../../README.md) is only an index and quick start. Who edits code/docs and in what order — [.cursor/rules/ai-companion.mdc](ai-companion.mdc).

## Best practices

- Follow [Effective Go](https://go.dev/doc/effective_go), [Go Code Review Comments](https://go.dev/wiki/CodeReviewComments) and the official guides on modules/packages.
- Packages — by responsibility and feature; public API — minimal, everything else unexported.
- Errors: wrap with context (`fmt.Errorf("...: %w", err)`), don't swallow; domain errors — in `domain`, mapping to HTTP — in `delivery/http`, join/admit on wire — in `delivery/errors` (see [.cursor/rules/join-error-opcodes.mdc](join-error-opcodes.mdc)). Before a new `fmt.Errorf("X not found")` — check whether an `ErrXxx` sentinel already exists in `domain`.
- `context.Context` — first argument in I/O and long-running operations; cancellation and deadlines — via `ctx`, not globals.
- Interfaces — on the consumer side (`port`), small; do not create "just in case". Configuration — not in `port`; see [.cursor/rules/config.mdc](config.mdc).
- Naming: short names in narrow scope; no `I` prefixes on interfaces; `ErrXxx` for sentinel errors; identifiers — ASCII only.
- Concurrency: explicit ownership transfer; `sync`/`errgroup` as needed; no goroutine leaks on shutdown. Channels written by producers outside the consumer's WaitGroup are **not closed** — the consumer exits on `ctx.Done`/done-channel, otherwise send-on-closed panic.
- Graceful shutdown is mandatory for any component with goroutines/resources: `Stop`/`Shutdown` cancels ctx → stops accepting new work → `wg.Wait()` for all spawned goroutines → only then returns/releases resources. Not fire-and-forget: whoever called `Stop` must see actual completion, not just a signal of it.
- Registry lifecycle invariants (P20): (1) teardown/replacement in the registry (`r.peers`, slots) — **identity-scoped** by object, not by bare key — an entry is deleted/mutated only if `map[key] == passed object`, otherwise sentinel (`ErrPeerStale` pattern); an old teardown after replace must not kill the new object under the same key. (2) An object **irreversibly evicted** from the registry must be stopped on all exit branches (success, mutation failure, rollback failure) — otherwise goroutines/conn live outside the registry: leak + ghost input. (3) Side effects (metrics, marks, broadcast) — **after** the authoritative lifecycle result, not before: a mark/metric on a live object from a stale operation is a false record; a pre-check of identity before the side effect narrows the race, the authoritative check is at the serialization point.
- Encapsulation / ownership of third-party library objects: do not hold a reference to an object whose lifetime belongs to someone else's scope (request-scoped wrappers, `sync.Pool`, handler-callback) beyond that scope. For session-scoped work take the inner object or a copy at entry (example: `WsConnection` holds `*fasthttp/websocket.Conn`, not the pooled contrib `*websocket.Conn`, which `releaseConn` resets after the handler returns).
- Binary/buffered I/O: reading exactly N bytes — `io.ReadFull`; `buf.Read` may return fewer without error → silent corruption.
- Dependencies: only needed ones; versions — via `go.mod` / `go get`, no copying into `vendor/` without reason.
- Linters and format: `go fmt`, `go vet`; if present in the repo — `staticcheck` / `golangci-lint`. Format only touched files — a repo-wide `goimports`/`gofmt -l` sweep (import regrouping in other people's files) is a separate commit on request, not part of the task.

## HTTP — Fiber

- For HTTP **only** [Fiber](https://gofiber.io/) (`github.com/gofiber/fiber/v3`).
- Forbidden: `net/http` handlers, Echo, Gin, Chi, mux and other HTTP frameworks.
- Fiber — in `internal/delivery/http/` (router, handlers, middleware).
- Handlers are thin: request parsing → use case → response/error mapping. No business logic in handlers and middleware.
- HTTP tests — in `tests/delivery/http/` via `fiber.App` and `app.Test` (or httptest over Fiber).

## Tests — only in `tests/`

- Do **not** put `*_test.go` next to production code in `cmd/`, `internal/`, `pkg/`.
- All tests — under the **`tests/`** directory, mirroring the code structure:

```text
tests/
  domain/...          # domain unit tests
  usecase/...         # scenario unit tests (port mocks)
  adapter/...         # adapter integration
  delivery/...        # HTTP / transport (Fiber app.Test etc.)
  integration/...     # end-to-end scenarios
```

- Test package — **external**: `package room_test`, import `github.com/Sorrowful-free/z-core-frontier-rooms/...`.
- Table-driven tests, `t.Parallel()` where safe; helpers — `t.Helper()`.
- Run: `go test ./tests/...` (from module root). CI equivalent: `go test -race -shuffle=on -count=1 ./tests/...` — run when flakes/races are suspected and before pushing. Failed CI logs: `gh run list` → `gh run view <id> --log-failed`.
- Port mocks: **mockgen** (`go.uber.org/mock`), generation into `tests/mocks/` — `go generate ./tests/mocks/...`; in tests `gomock.Controller`, do not duplicate hand-written stubs of `port/` interfaces.
- After finishing a feature — ask: **"Should tests be written for this feature?"** Do not write tests silently.

## Example

```go
// tests/usecase/room/create_test.go
package room_test

import (
	"testing"

	usecaseroom "github.com/Sorrowful-free/z-core-frontier-rooms/internal/usecase/room"
)
```
